          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An ordinal analysis of a formal system precisely measures its proof-theoretic strength, which is its strength at justifying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+induction"} transfinite induction
          %p
            Giving a concrete “ordinal notation” that describes the proof-theoretic strength of a system is sometimes seen as providing a constructive justification for the reasoning principles of the system. However, ordinal analysis is not limited to systems that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive"} constructive
            in the sense of not assuming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h3#relation_to_predicativity Relation to Predicativity
          %p
            While it is classically true that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+system#FormalSystems"} effective formal system
            has some limit to its proof-theoretic strength, only systems of limited strength have had their strength concretely described by a good ordinal notation. Specifically, all systems with ordinal analyses so far are significantly weaker than full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-order+arithmetic"} second-order arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative"} predicative
            systems than like familiar impredicative systems such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order arithmetic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %p Many have argued, however, that the boundary between predicatively-justifiable and impredicative systems lies somewhere within the range of proof-theoretic ordinals already analyzed. In other words, that the stronger systems in the table below are actually slightly impredicative, even when it isn’t obvious.
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong proof-theoretic ordinal
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is (commonly) defined as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mi sup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} otyp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mo ≺
                    %mtext is primitive recursive and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} TI
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {\vert T\vert} = \sup\{ \mathrm{otyp}({\prec}) \mid {\prec}\text{is primitive recursive and}\;
                    T \vdash \mathrm{TI}({\prec},X) \}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} otyp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{otyp}({\prec})
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-type"} order-type
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} TI
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ≺
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{TI}({\prec},X)
              means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (a free parameter) satisfies transfinite induction along
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≺
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              is well-ordered. For most theories, this ordinal is equal to the
              = succeed "-ordinal" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^1_1
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi sup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext is a
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 1
                    %mtext -sentence and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {\vert T\vert}_{\Pi^1_1} := \sup\{ \mathrm{tc}(F) \mid F\;\text{is a}\;\Pi^1_1\text{-sentence and }\;
                    T \vdash F \}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{tc}(F)
              is the truth-complexity of the
              = succeed "-sentence" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^1_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              (if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a first-order theory, we interpret
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi X
                    %mo .
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \vdash \forall X.F(X)
              as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \vdash F(X)
              with a free set parameter
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The proof-theoretic ordinal is always a recursive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt\omega_1^{\mathrm{CK}}
            ) if the theory is recursive.
          %p
            Note that the proof-theoretic ordinal is a rather blunt invariant, because if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is any true
            = succeed "-sentence," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^1_1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi T
                      %mo +
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\vert T\vert}_{\Pi^1_1} = {\vert T+F\vert}_{\Pi^1_1}
          %p
            So this tells us nothing about the provable arithmetical sentences of
            = succeed "!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            The solution is to refine the analysis to give a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^0_2
            ordinal analysis of the theory, using a measure of the computational complexity of provable
            = succeed "-sentences/provably" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^0_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^0_2
            ordinal should be thought of as an ordinal notation, rather than an actual ordinal). But for all the ordinal analyses we have so far, these choices turn out not to matter, basically because any
            %em natural ordinal notation system
            will give the same result. A major issue is, however, that we have no definition of what a natural ordinal notation system is (but we know one when we see it!).
          %p
            All of the ordinal analyses mentioned below also give a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^0_2
            ordinal analysis, and thus control of the computational complexity of the provably recursive functions.
          %h2#methods Methods
          %h3#upper_bounds Upper bounds
          %p
            Most ordinal analyses proceed by embedding the theory in an infinitary proof calculus deriving judgments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi α
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash^\alpha_\rho F
            meaning
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            has an infinitary derivation of height less than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            using only cuts of rank less than
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi α
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mn 0
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mi ρ
                      %mi α
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash^\alpha_\rho \Delta \Rightarrow \vdash^{\varphi\rho\alpha}_0 \Delta
          %p
            One sets up the calculus so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mn 0
                    %mi α
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash^\alpha_0 F
            implies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{tc}(F)\lt\alpha
          %h3#lower_bounds Lower bounds
          %p
            Lower bounds are usually done by directly deriving
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} TI
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \vdash \mathrm{TI}({\prec_n},X)
            for a sequence of primitive recursive well-orderings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec_n
            whose order-types approximate the proof-theoretic ordinal. We get these primitive recursive well-orderings from good ordinal notation systems, see below.
